{
  "version": 1,
  "questionId": "t01-q01",
  "speechText": "Basically, Node.js runs JavaScript on the main event loop. For I O work, such as a database or network request, Node.js can wait for the result without blocking the main thread. When the result is ready, the callback continues. With async and await, the current function pauses while waiting, but the server can still handle other work. Some file and DNS operations can also use the libuv worker pool. CPU-heavy work is different. If I run a heavy calculation on the main thread, it can block the event loop, so other requests have to wait. For CPU-heavy work, I can use worker threads, a background queue, or a separate service, depending on the use case. So overall, Node.js works very well for I O-heavy systems, but I avoid running heavy CPU work directly on the main thread.",
